{\*\ftnsep\chftnsep}\pgndec\pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1033\kerning1\dbch\af9\langfe2052\sl276\slmult1\ql\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\hich\af4\loch\fs22\lang1033\f4\loch

\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1033\kerning1\dbch\af9\langfe2052\sl276\slmult1\ql\sb0\sa200\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\fs22\lang1033\f4\loch
Since the map is not going to be infinite there needs to be defined edges.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1033\kerning1\dbch\af9\langfe2052\sl276\slmult1\ql\sb0\sa200\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\fs22\lang1033\f4\loch
Two edge types can be impossibly large cliffs/mounts that the crystal has no hope of ever getting over before killing you or the ocean, which the crystal cannot spread over.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1033\kerning1\dbch\af9\langfe2052\sl276\slmult1\ql\sb0\sa200\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\fs22\lang1033\f4\loch
The map doesn't need to be any defined shape, just as long as the play area is confined. Granted, leaning toward more sqaure shapes is ideal as it allows us to place the crystals starting point in any corner with consistancy. We don't want players to restart if they get a bad shape/spawn point.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1033\kerning1\dbch\af9\langfe2052\sl276\slmult1\ql\sb0\sa200\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\fs22\lang1033\f4\loch
The maps natural subdivsions will be biomes. Biomes can be sperated by features but it is not a requirement. This means that biome blending will be needed to some degree or we need to make sure biomes make sense next to each other... maybe. We won't have to worry about height difference between biomes if we follow the average height rule.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1033\kerning1\dbch\af9\langfe2052\sl276\slmult1\ql\sb0\sa200\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\fs22\lang1033\f4\loch
I personally don't what a lot of biomes per map, maybe 5-6 at most. The reason being is that the more biomes the more shit needs to be considered per map by both the player playing and us programing it. If we want to have hot and cold biomes wtihin one map thats another layer of gen we need to add.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1033\kerning1\dbch\af9\langfe2052\sl276\slmult1\ql\sb0\sa200\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\fs22\lang1033\f4\loch
Therefore I propose that we instead have the map roll an overall tempature on generation. Anywhere from hot, mild, cold. This means that the theme of the map will either be hot, mild, or cold across it's entirety.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1033\kerning1\dbch\af9\langfe2052\sl276\slmult1\ql\sb0\sa200\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\fs22\lang1033\f4\loch
A hot map would have dunes, badlands, canyons(as a biome), and eucalypt}{\hich\af4\loch\fs22\lang1033\f4\loch
i}{\hich\af4\loch\fs22\lang1033\f4\loch
s forest. Think an Australia map}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1033\kerning1\dbch\af9\langfe2052\sl276\slmult1\ql\sb0\sa200\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\fs22\lang1033\f4\loch
A mild map would have a plains, steppe, mountain, and temperate forest. Think europe.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1033\kerning1\dbch\af9\langfe2052\sl276\slmult1\ql\sb0\sa200\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\fs22\lang1033\f4\loch
A cold map would have snow, tundra, mountain(though snowwy), and tiagas. Northen Russia.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1033\kerning1\dbch\af9\langfe2052\sl276\slmult1\ql\sb0\sa200\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\fs22\lang1033\f4\loch
At the same time we can do things like hot/mild maps were both biome temps are present, though not their extremes. This massively increases replayability and makes each map temp a different experience. }
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang1033\kerning1\dbch\af9\langfe2052\sl276\slmult1\ql\sb0\sa200\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\fs22\lang1033\f4\loch
This does mean we need to have multiple biomes of different temps with the same height frequency but we already kinda do.}
\par }